  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nToday - Thursday: Widespread Rainfall\n\nAn upper level low is expected to develop across the Desert \nSouthwest this morning after becoming cutoff from a longwave \ntrough across the western United States. After this cutoff \noccurs, the longwave trough continues eastward across the \nnorthern United States and knock's on the Midwest's door this \nafternoon. Ahead of these features, southerly flow advects \ntropospheric deep moisture across the Central Plains into the \nMidwest, characterized by PWATs of 1.5-1.75 inches. The deep \nmoisture and forcing associated with the upper level features \nwill spark widespread moderate to heavy rainfall beginning this \nafternoon which largely continues through Thursday. The main \ncaveat is that a break in the rainfall looks to occur Wednesday \nmorning behind the trough and ahead of the cutoff low which \nbegins to lift northward towards the Midwest on Wednesday, \nbringing another round of heavy rainfall Wednesday afternoon \ninto Thursday.\n\nStill some uncertainty with regard to where the axis of \nheaviest rainfall sets-up through midweek. The ECMWF suite \n(ECMWF/EPS/AIFS) have been very consistent with more southward \nsolutions, putting northeast Iowa into southwest Wisconsin most \nat risk for higher rainfall amounts. In comparison, the GFS \nsuite (GFS/GEFS/AIGFS) has been more variable in the location of\nthis heavy rain axis having favored a northward solution 24 \nhours ago, but has begun to trend southward towards the ECMWF \nsolutions in recent runs. Currently thinking northeast Iowa into\nsouthwest Wisconsin are the most likely areas to see the \nhighest rainfall totals given these trends, especially for those\nalong and south of Highway 18. However, where this axis sets-up\nmay still change further as there is the potential for the \nheaviest rainfall to largely remain south of the area and across\ncentral into eastern Iowa (20-30%). This is seen in the 28.12z \nLREF cluster analysis, but the EFI has also largely kept the \ngreatest signal for anomalous rainfall potential to our south.\n\nRegardless of where the heavy rainfall axis sets-up, currently \nexpect the heaviest rainfall to occur Wednesday afternoon into \nWednesday night as a strengthening 850hPa low level jet surges \nanother plume of moisture northward, this time associated with \nthe remnants of Hurricane Polo as it gets absorbed into the mean\nflow when the cutoff low across the Southwest lifts northward. \nRAP/HRRR soundings also suggest some elevated instability may be\npresent during this period, generally around 300 J/kg, leading \nto some convective enhancement and locally higher rainfall \ntotals. Current probabilities for amounts of 2+\" sit around \n40-60% in the 29.01z NBM with the potential for amounts of 3-4+\"\nwhere convective enhancement occurs. Flooding concern remains \nthrough Thursday across portions of northeast Iowa, southwest \nWisconsin, and southeast Minnesota, especially if higher end \nrainfall totals are realized. Urban and street flooding along \nwith river rises are the main concerns although flash flooding \nis possible, especially where showers and storms frequent in \nrapid succession. Rainfall ends Thursday as a cold front moves \nthrough the region and high pressure slides southeastward into \nthe Midwest.\n\nFriday into the Weekend: Drier and Trending Cooler\n\nIn the wake of the cold frontal passage, temperatures should \nslowly trend cooler this upcoming weekend as broad scale \ntroughing attempts to dig south over the Great Lakes region. \nDaytime highs are not forecast to be all that much cooler, with \ntemperatures remaining in the mid 60s. Where the cooler air will\nbe more noticeable will be overnight, with overnight lows drop \ninto the lower 40s to mid 30s. Dry conditions are currently \nexpected but there may be a low chance for light rain on \nSaturday if moisture moves in quick enough out ahead of another \nsouthern surge in the upper level trough.",
